- How would you test memory pressure and eviction correctness?
- How would you handle out-of-order fraud_rules updates across partitions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a real-world KCNA pattern: dynamic routing controlled by a side-input, stateful enrichment with TTL, and delivery guarantees in the presence of config changes.

## Key Concepts

- State stores and TTL caches
- Side-input config streams
- Per-message offsets and at-least-once delivery
- ID-based deduplication across routing versions

## Code Example

```javascript
class RouterSink {
  constructor() {
    this.cache = new TTLCache({ ttl: 5 * 60 * 1000 }); // 5 minutes
  }

  onMessage(ctx, event) {
    const cached = this.cache.get(event.type);
    const route = cached?.route || 'default';

    emit({
      id: event.id,
      type: event.type,
      payload: event.payload,
      route,
      ts: event.ts
    });
  }

  onConfigUpdate(ctx, routingConfig) {
    for (const rule of routingConfig.rules) {
      this.cache.set(rule.eventType, { 
        route: rule.route, 
        version: routingConfig.version 
      });
    }
  }
}
```

