## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time access control in a multi-tenant KCNA deployment with minimal downtime and strong auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Live policy engine design
- Token lifecycle and revocation mechanisms
- Cross-region propagation and consistency
- Tamper-evident audit proofs and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-token validation sketch
function validateToken(token, tenantId, currentTime) {
  const payload = decodeAndVerify(token);
  if (!payload || payload.tenantId !== tenantId) return false;
  if (payload.exp < currentTime) return false;
  if (isRevoked(tenantId, payload.jti)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would clock skew and revocation storms be handled?
- How would you simulate cross-region revocation latency with mixed workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates deep understanding of per-tenant data isolation, external KMS integration, and verifiable auditing in a streaming system. Tests handling of key rotation, revocation, and tamper-evidence without leaking data.

## Key Concepts
- CMEK-based envelope encryption with per-tenant DEKs
- External KMS integration and key-wrapping
- Zero-downtime rotation and revocation workflows
- Tamper-evident auditing using Merkle proofs
- Tenant-to-key metadata, APIs, and test strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: obtain per-tenant DEK, encrypt, store ciphertext with key reference
async function ingestEvent(tenantId, plaintext) {
  const dek = await kms.getTenantDEK(tenantId);
  const iv = crypto.randomBytes(12);
  const ciphertext = crypto.aesGCM(plaintext, dek.key, iv);
  await storeCiphertext(tenantId, ciphertext, iv, dek.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you coordinate DEK rotation with in-flight data?
- How would you prove key usage for auditors without exposing payloads?

